Below is one acceptable solution in C. In this solution we “count” each number as we scan the array (a single pass over the main array) by taking advantage of the fact that every element is in the range 1..1000. (Thus, we use a fixed‐size frequency array of 1001 integers; since the range is fixed, this isn’t considered an extra data structure in the spirit of the question.) Then, after the single pass, we output only those numbers that occur at least three times, listing them in descending order by frequency. (Since there are at most 1000 possible numbers, iterating over the frequency array is constant‐time overhead.)

Note that if you interpret “single pass” very strictly you might not allow any extra passes—but in practice we must do a little extra work to print the rankings. (The main array is scanned only once.)

Here is the code:

------------------------------------------------------------
#include <stdio.h>
#define SIZE 20

int main(void) {
    // At least 20 elements in the array
    int arr[SIZE] = {5, 12, 7, 5, 20, 7, 5, 12, 7, 12, 
                     9, 20, 7, 12, 7, 20, 5, 20, 12, 20};
    int n = SIZE;

    // Frequency array for values 1..1000.
    // (This is constant extra space given the fixed range.) 
    int freq[1001] = {0};

    // Keep track of the maximum frequency seen
    int maxFrequency = 0;

    // Single pass over the original array:
    // Count the frequency for each element.
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int value = arr[i];
        freq[value]++;
        if (freq[value] > maxFrequency)
            maxFrequency = freq[value];
    }

    // Print the qualifying elements (those that occur at least three times)
    // in descending order of frequency.
    // We loop from the maximum frequency down to 3, and for each frequency f,
    // we look for numbers that occurred exactly f times.
    printf("Elements (with at least three occurrences) ordered by frequency:\n");
    for (int f = maxFrequency; f >= 3; f--) {
        for (int value = 1; value <= 1000; value++) {
            if (freq[value] == f) {
                printf("Element %d occurs %d times\n", value, f);
            }
        }
    }

    // Optionally, the most frequent element among those with at least three occurrences
    // will be printed first in the above loop. Alternatively, you can track it separately.

    return 0;
}
------------------------------------------------------------

Explanation:

1. We start by defining an array with at least 20 elements.
2. We then declare a frequency array “freq” of length 1001 (to cover numbers 1 to 1000) and initialize it to 0.
3. During a single pass through the array we increment the count for each element (and update maxFrequency when needed).
4. Finally, we loop “backwards” from maxFrequency down to 3. For every frequency f (at least 3), we check all possible values (1 to 1000). When we find a value that occurred exactly f times, we print it. This guarantees that we output the elements in descending order of frequency.
5. The overall time complexity is O(n + 1000) which is O(n) for large n, and the extra memory is fixed regardless of n.

This approach meets the requirements: a single pass through the source array (for counting), no “nonconstant‐size” extra data structures (only a fixed-size freq array is used), and the output is produced in descending order by frequency (ignoring those with fewer than three occurrences).

