Below is one possible solution in JavaScript. In this solution the algorithm does the following:

1. It “walks” the nested (and possibly circular or sparse) array in a depth‐first order so that each element is “visited” in the order the array (and its nested subarrays) would be traversed. (Only those positions in the subarrays that really exist are counted.) In this way we “flatten” the traversal without actually flattening the array. During the DFS the function saves the “path” (an array of indices from the top level down) for the two target flattened positions (only the first occurrence is recorded even if duplicates occur).

2. It validates that both indices are within bounds. If not an error is thrown.

3. If the two indices are the same nothing changes, so a deep copy of the array is returned.

4. Otherwise the function deep‑clones the entire nested array while preserving the structure – including sparse indices and circular references. (A WeakMap is used to detect/copy circular references properly.)

5. Then the function uses the saved paths on the cloned structure to swap the two elements. (Only the two first “hits” in the DFS order are swapped.)

6. Finally, the new cloned array (with the two swapped values) is returned.

The overall time complexity is O(n) (one DFS pass plus deep‑cloning, each proportional to the number of encountered elements) and the extra space (for the clone and the DFS recursion) is O(n).

