Below is one way to implement Bubble Sort under the specified constraints. Note that although Bubble Sort has O(n²) worst‐case time complexity—which may be too slow in interpreted languages like Python for arrays of 100,000 elements—the following implementation meets the requirements (no extra data structures, no built‐in sort, O(1) extra space, and uses Bubble Sort). In performance-critical environments (for instance, sorting 100,000 elements in under 1 second), you’d typically use a compiled language with optimization (such as C or C++). Nonetheless, here are two implementations: one in Python (for clarity) and one in C++ (for performance).

────────────────────────────
Python Implementation:

def bubble_sort(arr):
    n = len(arr)
    # Traverse through all array elements
    for i in range(n - 1):
        swapped = False
        # Last i elements are already in place
        for j in range(n - i - 1):
            if arr[j] > arr[j + 1]:
                # Swap without using any extra data structure
                arr[j], arr[j + 1] = arr[j + 1], arr[j]
                swapped = True
        # If no two elements were swapped in the inner loop, then break early
        if not swapped:
            break

# Example usage:
if __name__ == "__main__":
    # Example input; in production this could be up to 100,000 elements.
    array = [64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90]
    bubble_sort(array)
    print("Sorted array is:", array)


────────────────────────────
C++ Implementation:

#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

void bubbleSort(vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = arr.size();
    // Traverse through all array elements
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        bool swapped = false;
        // Last i elements are already in place
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                // Swap the elements
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
        // If no two elements were swapped in inner loop, then break early for efficiency.
        if (!swapped)
            break;
    }
}

int main() {
    // Example usage; in practice, the vector could contain up to 100,000 elements.
    vector<int> arr = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
    bubbleSort(arr);
    cout << "Sorted array is: ";
    for (int num : arr) {
        cout << num << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;
    return 0;
}

────────────────────────────
Explanation of the Algorithm:

1. We iterate through the array repeatedly.
2. In each pass (the outer loop), we compare adjacent items and swap them if they are in the wrong order.
3. A flag (swapped) is used to detect if a pass made any swap. If no swap occurs, the algorithm terminates early since the array is already sorted. This optimization is particularly useful if the list becomes sorted before completing all passes.
4. The algorithm does not use any additional data structures and performs swaps in-place, meeting the O(1) auxiliary space requirement.
5. The worst-case time complexity remains O(n²), but the optimization sometimes results in a best-case complexity of O(n) if the array is already sorted.

────────────────────────────
Performance Note:

While these implementations meet the algorithmic constraints, Bubble Sort is generally inefficient for large arrays. Sorting 100,000 elements using Bubble Sort in Python is unlikely to complete in under 1 second due to the interpreter overhead. However, a highly optimized C++ implementation compiled with optimization flags (e.g., -O2 or -O3) can sometimes process many elements faster, though keep in mind that Bubble Sort remains impractical for large datasets in most cases. 

