Below is one complete solution in C++ that meets the requirements. In this solution:

• We read the number of elements and then the elements themselves. (For larger data sets the input method may need adjustment, but here we illustrate the logic.)
• We immediately check that every element is unique by inserting into an unordered_set. If a duplicate is found the program prints an error message and exits.
• We compute the mean and variance using Welford’s one‐pass algorithm (which is O(n) and numerically stable).
• We implement our own square root function (Newton’s method) since we are not allowed to use a built-in sqrt.
• Finally, we print the computed standard deviation with exactly 6 decimal places.

Here is the complete code:

------------------------------------------------
#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_set>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdio>

using namespace std;

// Custom square root function using Newton's method
double customSqrt(double number) {
    if (number < 0) {
        // Should not happen for variance
        return -1;
    }
    if (number == 0 || number == 1) {
        return number;
    }
    double tolerance = 1e-10; // error tolerance
    double guess = number;   // initial guess
    while (true) {
        double next_guess = 0.5 * (guess + number / guess);
        if ( (guess - next_guess < tolerance && guess - next_guess > 0) ||
             (next_guess - guess < tolerance && next_guess - guess > 0) )
        {
            break;
        }
        guess = next_guess;
    }
    return guess;
}

int main(){
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    // Read number of elements
    size_t n;
    cin >> n;

    // It is assumed that n >= 1
    vector<double> arr;
    arr.reserve(n);

    unordered_set<double> uniqueElements;
    double x;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++){
        cin >> x;

        // Validate uniqueness.
        if(uniqueElements.find(x) != uniqueElements.end()){
            cerr << "Error: Array contains duplicate elements." << "\n";
            return 1;
        }
        uniqueElements.insert(x);
        arr.push_back(x);
    }

    // Using Welford's one-pass algorithm for mean and variance
    double mean = 0.0;
    double M2 = 0.0;
    size_t count = 0;

    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++){
        count++;
        double delta = arr[i] - mean;
        mean += delta / count;
        double delta2 = arr[i] - mean;
        M2 += delta * delta2;
    }

    // Variance is M2 divided by count (population variance)
    double variance = M2 / count;
    double stdDev = customSqrt(variance);

    // Print the result with exactly 6 decimal places
    printf("%.6f", stdDev);

    return 0;
}

------------------------------------------------

Explanation of key parts:

1. Input and Uniqueness Check:
   • We store each input number in a vector and simultaneously check and insert into an unordered_set.
   • If a duplicate is detected, the program prints an error message and exits.

2. Calculating Mean and Variance:
   • We use Welford’s online algorithm to update the mean and the sum-of-squares differences (M2) in a single pass. 
   • The variance is then M2 divided by the number of elements.

3. Custom Square Root:
   • The customSqrt() function uses Newton’s iterative method (also known as the Babylonian method) to compute the square root without relying on any built‐in math function.
   • The loop iterates until the difference between successive approximations is less than a small tolerance.

4. Output:
   • The final standard deviation is printed using printf with a format string ensuring exactly 6 decimal places.

This solution is O(n) in time, validates uniqueness, handles both positive and negative numbers gracefully, and does not use any built-in standard deviation or sqrt functions.

