Below is one acceptable solution in C++ that reverses a string “in place” (i.e. it does not create another array or use any built‐in reverse function) in O(n) time and constant extra space. (Note that in C++ a std::string’s storage is mutable so we can swap characters without extra memory. In languages with immutable strings you’d have to work around that limitation.) This solution manually swaps characters from the beginning and end until the whole string is reversed. It comes with several unit tests (using simple assert statements) covering a variety of edge cases – including empty strings, strings with only special characters, palindromes, spaces, and strings with Unicode/emojis. (A word of caution: if your source string is encoded in UTF‑8, then reversing by swapping individual char bytes may break multi‑byte characters. For full Unicode “code‐point” reversal you would need to decode/encode properly. Here we assume each element in the string is a single “character” according to your program’s logic.) 

Below is the complete C++ code:

------------------------------------------------
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <cassert>

// Reverses a string in place in O(n) time and constant extra space.
void reverseString(std::string &str) {
    // Set two indices at beginning and end of the string.
    int left = 0;
    int right = static_cast<int>(str.length()) - 1;

    // Swap the characters until the middle of the string is reached.
    while (left < right) {
        // Swap characters without using any built-in reverse methods.
        char temp = str[left];
        str[left] = str[right];
        str[right] = temp;

        left++;
        right--;
    }
}

// Unit tests for the reverseString function.
void runTests() {
    // Test 1: empty string
    {
        std::string test = "";
        reverseString(test);
        assert(test == "");
    }

    // Test 2: single character (also a palindrome)
    {
        std::string test = "a";
        reverseString(test);
        assert(test == "a");
    }

    // Test 3: typical case with letters, digits, special characters, and whitespace
    {
        std::string test = "Hello, World! 123";
        // Expected reversed string manually computed.
        std::string expected = "321 !dlroW ,olleH";
        reverseString(test);
        assert(test == expected);
    }

    // Test 4: string with multiple consecutive spaces and leading/trailing whitespace.
    {
        std::string test = "  This  is   a test.  ";
        std::string expected = "  .tset a   si  sihT  ";
        reverseString(test);
        assert(test == expected);
    }

    // Test 5: string with only special characters.
    {
        std::string test = "!@#$%^&*()";
        std::string expected = ")(*&^%$#@!";
        reverseString(test);
        assert(test == expected);
    }

    // Test 6: already a palindrome.
    {
        std::string test = "madam";
        std::string expected = "madam";
        reverseString(test);
        assert(test == expected);
    }

    // Test 7: string with Unicode characters and emojis.
    // CAUTION: This approach treats each char as a code unit.
    // For full Unicode (UTF‑8) correctness with multi‑byte characters a more advanced method is needed.
    {
        std::string test = "Hi 😊, Привет!";
        // When reversed by char, we expect the sequence of bytes to be reversed.
        // In many cases this may not produce intelligible Unicode. However, if we assume
        // that our string is a sequence of logical “characters” (e.g., using a wstring in real Unicode code‐point code),
        // the expected result would be a reversed order. For demonstration we are using this example.
        // Here, we are simply checking that our function runs without error.
        reverseString(test);
        // Because reversing a UTF‑8 encoded string by char can produce garbled sequences,
        // we will not use assert(expected == test) here.
        // Instead, we simply print out the result for manual inspection.
        std::cout << "Reversed Unicode+Emoji string: " << test << std::endl;
    }

    std::cout << "All tests passed successfully." << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    runTests();
    return 0;
}

------------------------------------------------

Explanation:

1. The reverseString function uses two integer indices (left and right) to swap characters from opposite ends of the string.
2. The while loop runs in O(n/2) iterations which is O(n) overall.
3. Only a temporary char variable is used for swapping, so the space complexity is constant.
4. Several unit tests are provided to verify that the function handles an empty string, one‐character strings, strings with spaces and special characters, palindromes, and a “Unicode plus emoji” case.
5. (Note on Unicode: This code works on a per‑char basis. For a full Unicode‑aware solution, especially when using multi‑byte encodings such as UTF‑8, you’d need additional processing which may require extra space.)

This satisfies the conditions: it does not use any built‑in reverse() function or external libraries, it runs in O(n) time with constant additional space, and it addresses a variety of edge cases.

