Below is one acceptable solution in C++ that meets the requirements. Notice that no built‐in looping constructs (like for or while) appear; the “loop” is implemented entirely via recursion. In this version the prime test is optimized by (1) handling even numbers separately and (2) only testing odd divisors up to the square root of the candidate. (In order to avoid calling sqrt() all the time we compare divisor*divisor to candidate.) Each prime is printed on its own line.

─────────────────────────────
// C++ Code

#include <iostream>

// Recursive helper to test primality of a number using odd divisors.
// divisor is assumed to be an odd integer >= 3.
bool isPrimeHelper(int num, int divisor) {
    // If divisor*divisor > num then no factor exists, so num is prime.
    if (divisor * divisor > num)
        return true;

    // If num is divisible by divisor, it is not prime.
    if (num % divisor == 0)
        return false;

    // Recursively test the next odd divisor.
    return isPrimeHelper(num, divisor + 2);
}

// Determines if num is prime.
// Special-cases for numbers <= 2.
bool isPrime(int num) {
    if (num <= 1) return false;        // 0 and 1 are not prime.
    if (num == 2) return true;           // 2 is prime.
    if (num % 2 == 0) return false;      // even numbers greater than 2 are not prime.
    // Check odd divisors starting at 3.
    return isPrimeHelper(num, 3);
}

// Recursive "loop" to print the first 1000 primes.
// 'count' is the number of primes printed so far.
// 'candidate' is the current number to check.
void printPrimesRecursive(int count, int candidate) {
    if (count == 1000)
        return;  // Base case: have printed 1000 primes

    if (isPrime(candidate)) {
        std::cout << candidate << std::endl;  // Print prime on new line.
        // Recurse: increment count and move to the next candidate.
        printPrimesRecursive(count + 1, candidate + 1);
    } else {
        // Recurse without incrementing count.
        printPrimesRecursive(count, candidate + 1);
    }
}

int main() {
    // Start generating prime numbers beginning from 2.
    printPrimesRecursive(0, 2);
    return 0;
}

─────────────────────────────

Explanation:

1. The isPrime() function immediately handles the trivial cases:
   • Numbers ≤ 1 are not prime.
   • 2 is prime.
   • Even numbers (other than 2) are not prime.
2. For odd numbers greater than 2, the helper function isPrimeHelper() is used. It recursively checks each odd divisor starting at 3 and stops testing when the square of the divisor exceeds the candidate number.
3. The printPrimesRecursive() function simulates a loop by recursively calling itself. It takes two parameters: the current count of found primes and the candidate number to test.
4. The recursion stops when we have printed 1000 primes.
5. Each prime number is printed on a new line.

This solution has a time complexity of O(n√n) (since for each candidate it tests divisors up to roughly √n) and uses constant extra space aside from the recursion (and each recursion call uses a constant amount of space). It also minimizes the arithmetic operations performed during the prime checking process by skipping even divisibility and only checking odd numbers.

