Also, note that because the original tree must not be changed, we assume that the tree–link pointers (for the left, middle, and right children) remain intact and that we “piggy–back” the new pointers in extra fields.
 
The idea is to “convert” each subtree (using recursion) into a doubly linked list sorted in descending order (largest first) in place. Then we merge the lists (always “merging” two lists at a time in descending order) while skipping duplicate values. Finally, we “insert” the current node (making sure its parent field is set) into the merged list. (This procedure naturally “maintains” the fact that each node “remembers” the original tree parent.) 
 
One important extra requirement was to avoid any extra data structures. (We may use the recursive call stack and a few pointer‐variables but nothing like an array or hash table.) Furthermore, since the “merge” routines walk through the lists only once the overall time is as efficient as possible (it ends up being roughly O(n log n) if the merging is done “smartly” or O(n²) in the worst case – but note that without extra data structures there is little room for improvement).
 
