Explanation:

1. Authentication & Authorization:
   • A simple decorator (@require_auth) extracts username and password from request headers.
   • If credentials are missing or invalid, the API returns a 401 Unauthorized error.
   • The decorator also simulates roles by adding “role” information to the request if needed for further authorization logic.

2. Endpoints:
   • POST /api/v2/leak/general/:
     – Expects a JSON payload with “url” and “leak_id”.
     – Returns a 400 error if payload or required parameters are missing.
     – Creates a new leak entry and returns a 201 response with a success message.
   • GET /api/v2/leak/general/:
     – Returns a list of all leak entries.
   • GET /api/v2/leak/general/<leak_id>:
     – Looks for a leak by “leak_id” and returns a 404 error if not found.
   • DELETE /api/v2/leak/general/<leak_id>:
     – Deletes a leak by “leak_id” and returns a confirmation message or 404 if not found.

3. Error Handling:
   • 401 for unauthorized access is handled in the authentication decorator.
   • 400 is returned when the JSON payload or the required parameters are missing.
   • 404 errors are provided both via route logic and a global error handler.

This design meets the requirements for a RESTful API to manage leak information in a cybersecurity context while handling various user roles and common errors.

