Below is one example of a modular Flask + Socket.IO application that meets the requirements. In this example the code is split into logical sections (which you might separate into multiple files in a large project). The application:

• Initializes the Flask app and Socket.IO server in a dedicated create_app() function.  
• Registers the HTTP route(s) for serving the HTML page that displays the current vote totals.  
• Registers Socket.IO event handlers – notably, one for “submit vote” events that validates and updates the vote counts in a thread‐safe way, then broadcasts the new totals.  
• Uses a thread lock whenever vote totals are updated to avoid race conditions.  
• Validates only “yes”, “no”, and “maybe” votes and sends user‑friendly error messages back to the client if the input is invalid.  
• Provides an HTML page (using Jinja2’s autoescaping) that shows the current vote totals and includes a form for voting. It also connects to the Socket.IO server so that vote totals update in real time.  
• Contains unit tests for the vote processing logic.

