Explanation of key components:

1. calendar.proto  
   • Defines the Reminder message and the CalendarService with a RecieveData method.

2. CalendarServicer.RecieveData  
   • Validates the incoming Reminder. It uses a regular expression to check email format and converts the date strings using datetime.fromisoformat.  
   • If validation passes, it calls schedule_reminder() to add the reminder to a priority queue.  
   • On any errors (invalid email, wrong date format, or past reminder time), it returns a ReminderResponse with success=False and an appropriate message.

3. schedule_reminder()  
   • Verifies that the reminder time is in the future and then adds the reminder to the global queue (sorted by reminder time).

4. reminder_dispatcher()  
   • Runs in a separate daemon thread. It continually checks the scheduled reminders. When the current time reaches or passes a reminder’s date_remind, it “sends” an email by calling send_email().  
   • If an error occurs while sending the email, report_error() is invoked.

5. serve()  
   • Sets up and starts the gRPC server on port 50051, registers the CalendarServicer, and starts the background reminder dispatcher thread.

This design meets the following requirements:
• It receives and validates incoming reminder data (including checking email format).  
• It schedules the reminder and “sends an email” at the specified time.  
• It handles errors (both in data validation and email sending) gracefully by reporting them to a dummy error handling service.

In a full production system you would replace the dummy email sending and error reporting functions with real implementations and possibly persist scheduled reminders in a database.

